{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"colossal-ghastful-marten","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n\nThe completion close path pins a ref without fetching, then validates that pin against itself. A tree that is out of date passes its own drift check by construction, so a `completion-verifier` signature can be minted over a tree that is not what landed.\n\nThe sequence, confirmed by reading the current code:\n\n1. `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:128-143` — `_pin_completion_ref(None)` resolves the default ref via `resolve_ref(\"HEAD\", fetch=False)`.\n2. `src/rebar/_commands/close_autoresume.py:259-279` — `verify_with_auto_resume` calls `llm.verify_completion(..., source=\"attested\", ref=(ref or \"HEAD\"), fetch=False, ...)`, verifying at that pin.\n3. `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:310-323` — the post-verify signing drift check **re-resolves that same pinned SHA**, again with `fetch=False`.\n\nStep 3 is the defect. The check that exists to catch the tree moving under the verification reads the identical local `HEAD` that step 1 read, so it compares the tree against itself. There is no state in which it can report drift caused by the local tree being behind the remote. `src/rebar/opcert_service/jobs.py:176-187` pins the same way on the op-cert path.\n\nClaim-time currency has the same shape though not the same consequence: `src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:89-109` resolves `current_head_sha` with `fetch=False`, so it compares against a *local* idea of `origin/main` rather than the remote's actual tip.\n\n## Why this is separate from 8806\n\n`8806-198c-e2d6-46ce` is a deployment defect: the MCP code workspace is cloned once and never refreshed. Its fix advances that checkout. This ticket is a verification defect: a drift check that cannot detect drift. They were found together, and one makes the other visible, but neither fixes the other — a correctly refreshed workspace still has a drift check that compares a tree against itself, and a working drift check still cannot help a workspace nothing ever advances. Fixing only 8806 would leave a signature path that is sound only as long as no deployment is ever stale again.\n\n## Status of the exploit\n\n**The mechanism is demonstrated. An actual false PASS is not.** The one incident observed in the wild produced a false FAIL — the verifier could not see work that had merged — which is the safe direction. No attestation has been shown to have been minted over a stale tree.\n\nTwo signatures are known to have been produced only after a workspace was manually fast-forwarded to the SHA they name: `f920-dc37-28e4-4a91` at `verified-at-sha:e5293bf5b1`, and `c65c-931c-ee39-4e63` at `verified-at-sha:e46d03aa83`. Those are consistent with correct verification against a freshly advanced tree; they are not evidence of a false PASS. `f920` separately carries a plan-review op-cert whose signed manifest anchors `verified-at-sha:5c2d3e664` while its attestation plaintext `head_sha` reads `665d1d0480`, and `plan_review_status` reports `stale-code` for it — but that verdict is itself computed from the local gate ref rather than a remote fetch.\n\nEstablishing whether any live attestation was minted over a stale tree requires a store-wide `verified-at-sha` census. One was attempted and abandoned for runtime. That census is the first deliverable here, because the answer decides whether this is a latent hole or an incident.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. **Census first.** Walk every `completion-verifier` and plan-review attestation in the store, extract its `verified-at-sha`, and determine whether that SHA was an ancestor of the branch tip at the time it was signed. Make it resumable, because the previous attempt died on runtime. Report any attestation whose signed SHA was not reachable from the branch it claimed to verify.\n2. **Make the drift check capable of detecting drift.** It must compare the verified tree against the authoritative remote tip, not against the same local pin it already read. Decide explicitly whether that means a fetch inside the check, or carrying the remote tip observed at pin time through to the check — the second avoids a network call in the signing path but only detects movement, not staleness at pin time.\n3. **Fail closed.** If the authoritative tip cannot be determined, the close refuses and emits no signature. It must not fall back to the self-comparison, which is this defect restated.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nTDD, failing test first.\n\n- A test pins a local `HEAD` that is behind the remote tip, runs the close path, and asserts no signature is produced. This must fail against today's code, which signs.\n- A test asserts a close against a current tree still signs, so the fix is not \"always refuse\".\n- A test asserts an unreachable remote produces a refusal rather than a signature.\n- A test drives the census against a store containing one attestation whose SHA is unreachable from its branch, and asserts it is reported.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A close whose pinned tree is behind the authoritative remote tip produces no signature. Verified by a test that fails against today's self-comparing drift check.\n- [ ] A close against a current tree still produces a signature. Verified by a test asserting the fix does not refuse valid closes.\n- [ ] A close whose authoritative tip cannot be determined produces no signature. Verified by a test with an unreachable remote.\n- [ ] The census reports an attestation whose signed SHA was unreachable from the branch it claimed. Verified by a test store seeded with exactly that case.\n- [ ] The census is resumable across interruption. Verified by interrupting a run and restarting it without recomputing completed work.\n- [ ] The census has been run against the live store and its result recorded on this ticket. Verified by reading that comment.\n- [ ] The fix requires no single CI provider. Verified by reading the trigger path for any GitHub Actions or Gerrit dependency.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nRefreshing the MCP code workspace, which is `8806-198c-e2d6-46ce`. The gates' verdict logic. Revoking or re-minting any attestation the census implicates — if the census finds one, that remediation is its own unit of work, because the response depends on what is found.","id":"a2e5-094d-0736-456e","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Completion close re-resolves its own pinned SHA with fetch=False, so the signing drift check compares a tree against itself and cannot detect staleness"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1789784902618078892,"uuid":"67d539ee-e94f-407d-a86b-322a3761f4a0"}